Report: CR/LF/CRLF normalization for finite character lists (adapted source)
1 claims; 0 removal(s).
  run_ids: ["4ee1dd0c-aadc-48c8-8e8d-8e2fd444a590"] -> ["06410edc-13b8-4479-858f-22f87bc77ad2"]
  explanation: "For every finite character list, the Aeneas translation of normalize-line-endings 0.3.0's adapted normalized constructor and Iterator::next returns exactly the list obtained by replacing each CRLF pair and each remaining CR with LF, while preserving other characters. The proof uses a total, fused list iterator and an observation budget greater than the input length, with no fixed input bound. The output contains no CR, is no longer than the input, and is unchanged by another normalization pass. The constructor and next bodies are dependencies of the checked public theorem. Extraction required replacing three character patterns with equality guards. Kani additionally checks one-step equivalence of the actual original and adapted next bodies for both prior-state flags and all possible character replies in a non-panicking two-reply iterator trace model: returned value, adapter state, callback count, and remaining modeled iterator state agree. The Aeneas theorem applies to the adapted extraction." -> "For every finite character list, the Aeneas translation of normalize-line-endings 0.3.0's adapted normalized constructor and Iterator::next returns exactly the list obtained by replacing each CRLF pair and each remaining CR with LF, while preserving other characters. The proof uses a total, fused list iterator and an observation budget greater than the input length, with no fixed input bound. The output contains no CR, is no longer than the input, and is unchanged by another normalization pass. The constructor and next bodies are dependencies of the checked public theorem. Extraction required replacing three character patterns with equality guards. Kani additionally checks one-step equivalence of the actual original and adapted next bodies for both prior-state flags and all possible character replies in a non-panicking two-reply iterator trace model: returned value, adapter state, callback count, and remaining modeled iterator state agree. The Aeneas theorem applies to the adapted extraction. Separate original-source evidence now proves the unchanged normalized constructor directly with official Aeneas: for every translated iterator and Iterator dictionary it returns that iterator and initializes the previous-CR flag to false. This says nothing about later next calls. Independently, Kani checks original iterator outputs for lengths zero through four and every valid Unicode scalar value against an eager CR/LF/CRLF reference: exact output, no CR, nonincreasing length, idempotence, two exhausted None replies, and the initial inherited Iterator size_hint default. The Kani input iterator is total and finite; unwind bound eight retains unwinding assertions (437 successful checks). These original-source supplements are distinct from the one adapted-source machine claim."
  evidence_url: "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/98335a1180275e0c677b6978679e8cc65d95466b" -> "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/8c082b067821b7f30bed4172a37e0307c921c9bb"
  limitations: "The original crates.io source is retained unchanged, but its character-pattern switches are unsupported by the pinned Aeneas translator. The three guard replacements have a Kani-checked one-step equivalence under the documented two-reply, non-panicking callback model. This is not a generic contextual-equivalence proof for every Rust Iterator implementation, nor a direct Aeneas proof of the unmodified release. The end-to-end Aeneas guarantee remains scoped to the adapted extraction and total fused list iterator model. Arbitrary external iterators, their panics or divergence, and iterators that resume after None are outside the end-to-end theorem. No separate general memory-safety claim is made." -> "The original crates.io source is retained unchanged, but its character-pattern switches are unsupported by the pinned Aeneas translator. The three guard replacements have a Kani-checked one-step equivalence under the documented two-reply, non-panicking callback model. This is not a generic contextual-equivalence proof for every Rust Iterator implementation, nor a direct arbitrary-length Aeneas proof of the unmodified iterator next body. The original constructor alone is separately body-proved; bounded original iterator Kani behavior does not establish arbitrary-length or arbitrary-callback behavior. The end-to-end Aeneas guarantee remains scoped to the adapted extraction and total fused list iterator model. Arbitrary external iterators, their panics or divergence, and iterators that resume after None are outside the end-to-end theorem. No separate general memory-safety claim is made."
